WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.292
Welcome to Part 3: wire,
your kit, and being ready.

2
00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:09.924
By the end, you'll know which wire
goes where, how to keep water out,

3
00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:15.510
why long runs lose volts, and exactly what
you need before your first job of the day.

4
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.182
The weakest wire in the system is the
20 gauge built into the light string.

5
00:00:21.182 --> 00:00:25.653
We can't change it. Everything we
run up to it is there to protect it.

6
00:00:25.953 --> 00:00:29.205
With wire, a thicker wire
has a smaller number.

7
00:00:29.505 --> 00:00:33.392
16 gauge is our feeder.
It carries 4 amps easily.

8
00:00:33.392 --> 00:00:36.894
18 gauge is also fine on a 4 amp circuit.

9
00:00:37.194 --> 00:00:42.278
14 gauge works, but it's more than we
need. Use up the stock, and don't buy more.

10
00:00:42.278 --> 00:00:48.429
Thicker feeder doesn't make the circuit safer,
because the 20 gauge string is still the limit.

11
00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:54.443
From the box to an injection,
the feeder is 16/2 or 18/2,

12
00:00:54.443 --> 00:00:57.423
FT-4 rated, and stranded copper.

13
00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:02.385
Never solid core. Solid copper
hardens, and snaps at the connections.

14
00:01:02.685 --> 00:01:06.805
Going through a wall, the
jacket has to have an ETL,

15
00:01:06.805 --> 00:01:11.016
CSA or UL number printed
on it. Check every spool.

16
00:01:11.316 --> 00:01:14.810
No printed number, and it
doesn't go through a wall.

17
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.016
No exceptions. Not sure?
Ask your crew lead.

18
00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:21.099
Next, keeping water out.

19
00:01:21.399 --> 00:01:24.506
A squirt of dielectric
grease in every connector,

20
00:01:24.506 --> 00:01:28.116
before you push it together.
It pushes the water out.

21
00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:32.051
The little O-ring is what seals
a light. Not the plastic body,

22
00:01:32.051 --> 00:01:37.519
and not how tight you make it. Over-tightening
just makes the light hard to change later.

23
00:01:37.819 --> 00:01:41.138
Lever nuts aren't waterproof.
They stay inside the box.

24
00:01:41.138 --> 00:01:43.139
Never outside in the weather.

25
00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:48.247
Never plug in with the power on.
Shut it off at the box first.

26
00:01:48.547 --> 00:01:54.766
On a run of more than 30 lights, plugging in live
kills the chip in the light before the one you touched.

27
00:01:54.766 --> 00:01:58.452
You won't see it happen. It
comes back later as a callout.

28
00:01:58.752 --> 00:02:03.380
So it's always the same order.
Power off. Plug in. Power on.

29
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:09.363
Long runs lose volts. The further
power travels, the more it drops.

30
00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:15.336
We tested 50 feet of 20 gauge.
In at 24.1 volts, out at 22.7.

31
00:02:15.336 --> 00:02:20.887
That's a 5.8 percent drop,
just past our 5 percent limit.

32
00:02:21.187 --> 00:02:24.207
So when the far-end
lights look dim or yellow,

33
00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:26.690
it's voltage drop, not bad lights.

34
00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.692
The fix is never a bigger
supply. It's a closer injection.

35
00:02:31.842 --> 00:02:35.903
Now, your kit. Before every job,
check that you've got all of it.

36
00:02:36.203 --> 00:02:40.507
Tools, charged and working. Drill
and driver batteries charged.

37
00:02:40.507 --> 00:02:44.583
Clamp meter and multimeter
working, with good batteries.

38
00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:48.757
Track. A proper supply for
every job on the schedule today,

39
00:02:48.757 --> 00:02:51.371
plus back track if the job needs it.

40
00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:56.350
Screws. Restocked, and the right
colour for each job on the schedule.

41
00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:00.905
Wire and parts. 16/2 FT-4
feeder, dielectric grease,

42
00:03:00.905 --> 00:03:05.738
spare 4 amp breakers, and
lever nuts for inside the box.

43
00:03:06.038 --> 00:03:11.152
And the job plan. Find the marked-up
photo and the field card in the job files.

44
00:03:11.452 --> 00:03:13.446
No meter, no install.

45
00:03:14.596 --> 00:03:17.852
Before every job, fill
out the pre-job checklist.

46
00:03:18.152 --> 00:03:20.915
It walks you through
the kit, item by item.

47
00:03:21.215 --> 00:03:24.137
Fill it out honestly, and
you'll be ready for the job.

48
00:03:25.287 --> 00:03:27.169
Site note. Being ready.

49
00:03:27.469 --> 00:03:30.030
Be ready to go, and
getting your kit together,

50
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.119
at least one hour before
your first job of the day.

51
00:03:33.419 --> 00:03:37.086
Dress for the weather. Layers,
gloves, and boots with grip.

52
00:03:37.086 --> 00:03:40.382
It's colder on a roof than
it is on the driveway.

53
00:03:40.682 --> 00:03:43.744
Check the schedule and the
job files the night before,

54
00:03:43.744 --> 00:03:45.809
so you know what each job needs.

55
00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:51.004
Phone charged. Ladders checked and
strapped down. Fall protection with you.

56
00:03:51.304 --> 00:03:55.188
Running late, or missing something?
Tell your crew lead right away.

57
00:03:55.188 --> 00:03:57.062
Not when you get to the job.

58
00:03:58.212 --> 00:03:59.236
Let's lock it in.

59
00:03:59.536 --> 00:04:03.112
Feeder is 16/2 or 18/2, FT-4, stranded.

60
00:04:03.112 --> 00:04:07.948
Through a wall, only with
a printed rating number.

61
00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:11.518
Grease every connector.
Lever nuts stay in the box.

62
00:04:11.518 --> 00:04:13.627
Power off before you plug in.

63
00:04:13.927 --> 00:04:18.899
Lights dim at the far end? Move the
injection closer, never a bigger supply.

64
00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:24.253
Kit checked, pre-job checklist filled
out, ready an hour before your first job.

65
00:04:24.553 --> 00:04:28.977
Next, take the Part 3 quiz.
Part 4 is how to wire it.
